<date>2026-11-08</date>
<releaseinfo>0.1.0</releaseinfo>

<abstract>
<para>
&tdefirewalld; is a graphical front end for the &firewalld; firewall daemon.
It is available as a Trinity Control Center module and as a standalone
application.
</para>
</abstract>

<keywordset>
<keyword>TDE</keyword>
<keyword>Trinity</keyword>
<keyword>firewall</keyword>
<keyword>firewalld</keyword>
<keyword>zone</keyword>
<keyword>polkit</keyword>
<keyword>kcontrol</keyword>
</keywordset>
</bookinfo>

<!-- ================================================================== -->

<chapter id="introduction">
<title>Introduction</title>

<para>
&tdefirewalld; lets you view and change the configuration of
&firewalld;, the dynamic firewall daemon used by Debian and many other
Linux distributions. It talks to &firewalld; directly over the D-Bus
system bus, the same interface that <command>firewall-cmd</command> and
<command>firewall-config</command> use.
</para>

<para>
With &tdefirewalld; you can:
</para>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>see whether the firewall is running and which zones are active;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>change the default zone, enable panic mode and logging of denied packets;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>allow services and open ports in a zone;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>set up masquerading (NAT) and port forwarding;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>add and remove rich rules;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>bind network interfaces and source addresses to zones;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>keep an eye on the firewall with an optional system tray icon;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>browse the definitions of all known services;</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>edit either the <emphasis>runtime</emphasis> or the <emphasis>permanent</emphasis> configuration.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The &tdefirewalld; status page</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="status.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The &tdefirewalld; status page</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->

<chapter id="getting-started">
<title>Getting Started</title>

<sect1 id="requirements">
<title>Requirements</title>

<itemizedlist>
<listitem><para>The <application>Trinity Desktop Environment</application> R14.1 or later.</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>&firewalld; 1.0 or later, installed and running
(<command>systemctl enable --now firewalld</command>).</para></listitem>
<listitem><para>A &polkit; authentication agent, normally
<application>polkit-agent-tde</application>, which is started automatically with
your TDE session.</para></listitem>
</itemizedlist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="starting">
<title>Starting &tdefirewalld;</title>

<para>
There are two ways to use &tdefirewalld;:
</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term>In the Trinity Control Center</term>
<listitem>
<para>
Open the <application>Trinity Control Center</application> and select
<menuchoice><guimenu>Security &amp; Privacy</guimenu><guimenuitem>Firewall</guimenuitem></menuchoice>.
The module can also be opened on its own with
<userinput><command>tdecmshell</command> <option>firewalld</option></userinput>.
</para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The firewall module in the Trinity Control Center</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="kcontrol.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The firewall module in the Trinity Control Center</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</listitem>
</varlistentry>

<varlistentry>
<term>As a standalone application</term>
<listitem>
<para>
Choose <guimenuitem>Firewall Manager</guimenuitem> in the
<guimenu>System</guimenu> section of the TDE menu, or run
<userinput><command>tde-firewalld</command></userinput>. See
<xref linkend="commands"/> for the command line options.
</para>
</listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>
Both offer exactly the same functionality. Changes are sent to &firewalld;
immediately; there is no <guibutton>Apply</guibutton> button.
</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="runtime-permanent">
<title>Runtime and Permanent Configuration</title>

<para>
&firewalld; keeps two sets of configuration, and the
<guilabel>Configuration</guilabel> selector at the top of the window
chooses which one you see and edit:
</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Runtime</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
The configuration that is active right now. Changes take effect
immediately but are lost when &firewalld; is reloaded or the computer
is restarted. Use it to try out changes.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Permanent</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
The configuration that is saved to disk (in
<filename>/etc/firewalld</filename>). Changes are not active until the
firewall is reloaded with <guibutton>Reload Firewall</guibutton>.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<para>
Two buttons act on both configurations:
</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Reload Firewall</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>
Loads the permanent configuration and makes it the runtime
configuration. Runtime-only changes are lost.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guibutton>Runtime to Permanent</guibutton></term>
<listitem><para>
Saves the current runtime configuration as the permanent
configuration. Use it after you have tested your changes.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Editing the permanent configuration</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="zones-permanent.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Editing the permanent configuration</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="authentication">
<title>Authentication</title>

<para>
Changing the firewall is a privileged operation. &tdefirewalld; itself
runs as your normal user and never runs as root. Instead, &firewalld;
asks &polkit; whether you are allowed to do what you requested. When
authentication is required, the &polkit; agent
(<application>polkit-agent-tde</application>) shows a dialog asking for
an administrator password.
</para>

<para>
The default &polkit; policies decide when you are asked. On Debian, the
<quote>server</quote> policy is installed by default: you need to
authenticate to read the zone details and again to change the
configuration. After a successful authentication &polkit; remembers it
for a few minutes.
</para>

<para>
If you cancel the password dialog, &tdefirewalld; shows a banner
<guilabel>Authentication is required to view the firewall
configuration</guilabel> and stops asking. Click
<guibutton>Authenticate...</guibutton> to try again.
</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Authentication was cancelled</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="auth-required.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Authentication was cancelled</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
If no &polkit; agent is running at all, a warning is shown at the top of
the window. Start <command>polkit-agent-tde</command> or log in to TDE
again.
</para>

<note><para>
Administrators can change the policy with &polkit; rules. For example,
to let members of the <systemitem class="groupname">netadmin</systemitem>
group change the firewall without a password, create
<filename>/etc/polkit-1/rules.d/50-firewalld.rules</filename>:
</para>
<programlisting>
polkit.addRule(function(action, subject) {
    if (action.id.indexOf("org.fedoraproject.FirewallD1.") == 0 &amp;&amp;
        subject.isInGroup("netadmin") &amp;&amp; subject.active) {
        return polkit.Result.YES;
    }
});
</programlisting>
</note>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->

<chapter id="using">
<title>Using &tdefirewalld;</title>

<sect1 id="status-page">
<title>The Status Page</title>

<para>
The <guilabel>Status</guilabel> page shows whether &firewalld; is running and
which version is installed. If &firewalld; is not running, a banner is shown
with a <guibutton>Start firewalld</guibutton> button, which asks systemd to start
the service (this also requires authentication).
</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Default zone</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
The zone used for all network connections and interfaces that are not
bound to a specific zone. Changing it takes effect immediately and is
also saved permanently.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Log denied packets</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
Whether packets rejected or dropped by the firewall are written to the
kernel log: <guilabel>Off</guilabel>, <guilabel>All</guilabel>, or only
<guilabel>Unicast</guilabel>, <guilabel>Broadcast</guilabel> or
<guilabel>Multicast</guilabel> packets.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Panic mode</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
Blocks <emphasis>all</emphasis> incoming and outgoing network traffic
and terminates active connections. Use it if you think your computer
is under attack. You are asked to confirm before it is enabled.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guilabel>Active Zones</guilabel></term>
<listitem><para>
The zones that currently have interfaces or source addresses bound to
them.
</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="zones-page">
<title>The Zones Page</title>

<para>
A zone describes how much you trust the network a connection is
attached to. &firewalld; comes with predefined zones, for example
<literal>public</literal> for untrusted networks, <literal>home</literal>
and <literal>work</literal> for networks you mostly trust,
<literal>trusted</literal> which accepts everything, and
<literal>drop</literal> and <literal>block</literal> which reject all incoming
connections.
</para>

<para>
Select a zone in the list on the left; the default zone is marked with
a star. The right side shows the zone's description, its
<guilabel>Target</guilabel> and several tabs.
</para>

<para>
The <guilabel>Target</guilabel> decides what happens to packets that
do not match any rule: <guilabel>Default</guilabel> rejects them but allows
ICMP, <guilabel>ACCEPT</guilabel> accepts them, <guilabel>DROP</guilabel>
drops them silently and <guilabel>REJECT</guilabel> rejects them. The
target can only be changed in the permanent configuration.
</para>

<sect2 id="zone-services">
<title>Services</title>
<para>
A service is a named set of ports and protocols, for example
<literal>ssh</literal> (port 22/tcp) or <literal>samba</literal>. Check a
service to allow incoming connections to it in the selected zone, and
uncheck it to block them again. The <guilabel>Ports</guilabel> column
shows what the service opens.
</para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Services allowed in the public zone</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="zones-services.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Services allowed in the public zone</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="zone-ports">
<title>Ports</title>
<para>
Opens additional ports that are not covered by a service. Click
<guibutton>Add...</guibutton>, enter a port number (for example
<userinput>8080</userinput>) or a range (for example
<userinput>6000-6010</userinput>) and choose the protocol
(<literal>tcp</literal>, <literal>udp</literal>, <literal>sctp</literal> or
<literal>dccp</literal>). To close a port, select it and click
<guibutton>Remove</guibutton>.
</para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Opening a port</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="add-port-dialog.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Opening a port</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="zone-masquerading">
<title>Masquerading</title>
<para>
Masquerading is IPv4 network address translation. Enable it in the zone
facing the Internet to let other machines use this computer as a
router. It is also needed for port forwarding to other hosts.
</para>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="zone-forwarding">
<title>Port Forwarding</title>
<para>
Forwards connections that arrive at a local port to another port on
this computer, or to a port on another host. Click
<guibutton>Add...</guibutton> and enter the source port, the protocol, and the
destination port and/or address.
</para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Port forwarding in the external zone</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="zones-port-forwarding.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Port forwarding in the external zone</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="zone-rich-rules">
<title>Rich Rules</title>
<para>
Rich rules express things the other tabs cannot, such as allowing a
service only from certain addresses, logging, or rate limiting. They
use the &firewalld; rich language described in
<citerefentry><refentrytitle>firewalld.richlanguage</refentrytitle><manvolnum>5</manvolnum></citerefentry>.
The <guibutton>Add...</guibutton> dialog contains several examples to start
from, for example:
</para>
<programlisting>rule family="ipv4" source address="192.168.1.0/24" service name="ssh" accept</programlisting>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Adding a rich rule</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="rich-rule-dialog.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Adding a rich rule</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</sect2>

<sect2 id="zone-interfaces">
<title>Interfaces and Sources</title>
<para>
The <guilabel>Interfaces</guilabel> tab lists the network interfaces bound
to the zone, and the <guilabel>Sources</guilabel> tab lists source addresses
whose traffic is handled by the zone. A source can be an IP address, a
network in CIDR notation (for example <userinput>10.8.0.0/24</userinput>), a
MAC address, or an ipset (<userinput>ipset:name</userinput>).
</para>
<note><para>
If an interface is managed by <application>NetworkManager</application>,
NetworkManager sets its zone from the connection settings, which can
override what you configure here.
</para></note>
</sect2>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="services-page">
<title>The Services Page</title>
<para>
The <guilabel>Services</guilabel> page lists every service definition known to
&firewalld;. Type in the <guilabel>Search</guilabel> field to filter the
list. Selecting a service shows its description, ports, protocols, helper
modules and destinations. The page is read-only; service definitions
are files in <filename>/usr/lib/firewalld/services</filename> and
<filename>/etc/firewalld/services</filename>.
</para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Browsing service definitions</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="services.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Browsing service definitions</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="interfaces-page">
<title>The Interfaces Page</title>
<para>
The <guilabel>Interfaces</guilabel> page shows every network interface of
the computer and the zone that handles its traffic. Interfaces without
an explicit zone use the default zone. Select an interface and click
<guibutton>Change Zone...</guibutton> (or double-click it) to move it to
another zone, or choose <guilabel>(default zone)</guilabel> to remove the
explicit binding.
</para>
<screenshot>
<screeninfo>Network interfaces and their zones</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="interfaces.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>Network interfaces and their zones</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="tray">
<title>The System Tray Icon</title>

<para>
<command>tde-firewalld-tray</command> puts a firewall icon into the system tray
of the <application>Kicker</application> panel. Its tooltip shows the firewall
state, the default zone and the active zones with their interfaces and
sources. The icon is greyed out when &firewalld; is not running and shows a
red stop sign while panic mode is on.
</para>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The tray icon's tooltip</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="tray-tooltip.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The tray icon's tooltip</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
Click the icon with the left mouse button to open the Firewall Manager. The
right mouse button opens a menu with:
</para>

<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Open Firewall Manager</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Starts <command>tde-firewalld</command>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guisubmenu>Default Zone</guisubmenu></term>
<listitem><para>Lists all zones; the current default zone is checked. Choosing
another zone makes it the default zone.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Panic Mode</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Turns panic mode on (after a confirmation) or off.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Reload Firewall</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Reloads the permanent configuration.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><guimenuitem>Start Automatically at Login</guimenuitem></term>
<listitem><para>Starts the tray icon with every TDE session.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>

<screenshot>
<screeninfo>The tray icon's menu</screeninfo>
<mediaobject>
<imageobject><imagedata fileref="tray-menu.png" format="PNG"/></imageobject>
<textobject><phrase>The tray icon's menu</phrase></textobject>
</mediaobject>
</screenshot>

<para>
The tray icon is <emphasis>not</emphasis> started automatically by default.
Start it once from the TDE menu (<guimenuitem>Firewall Tray</guimenuitem> in the
<guimenu>System</guimenu> section) or by running
<userinput><command>tde-firewalld-tray</command></userinput>, then check
<guimenuitem>Start Automatically at Login</guimenuitem> if you want it in every
session. The setting is stored as <literal>Autostart</literal> in the
<literal>[General]</literal> group of
<filename>~/.trinity/share/config/tde-firewalld-trayrc</filename>.
</para>

<para>
Just showing the icon never asks for a password: it only uses requests that
&firewalld; allows for every user. Changing the default zone, panic mode or
reloading asks for authentication like the Firewall Manager does.
</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->

<chapter id="commands">
<title>Command Reference</title>

<sect1 id="menus">
<title>Menus of the Standalone Application</title>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycap>F5</keycap></shortcut><guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Refresh View</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Reads the configuration from &firewalld; again.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Reload Firewall</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Same as the <guibutton>Reload Firewall</guibutton> button.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Runtime to Permanent</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Same as the <guibutton>Runtime to Permanent</guibutton> button.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><menuchoice><shortcut><keycombo action="simul">&Ctrl;<keycap>Q</keycap></keycombo></shortcut><guimenu>File</guimenu><guimenuitem>Quit</guimenuitem></menuchoice></term>
<listitem><para>Quits the application.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
<para>The <guimenu>Help</guimenu> menu is the standard TDE help menu.</para>
</sect1>

<sect1 id="command-line">
<title>Command Line Options</title>
<para>
Besides the standard Trinity and TQt options, <command>tde-firewalld</command>
accepts:
</para>
<variablelist>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--permanent</option></term>
<listitem><para>Start with the permanent configuration selected.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--page</option> <replaceable>page</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>Show the given page first: <literal>status</literal>,
<literal>zones</literal>, <literal>services</literal> or <literal>interfaces</literal>.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
<varlistentry>
<term><option>--zone</option> <replaceable>zone</replaceable></term>
<listitem><para>Select the given zone on the zones page.</para></listitem>
</varlistentry>
</variablelist>
<para>
See also the manual pages <citerefentry><refentrytitle>tde-firewalld</refentrytitle><manvolnum>1</manvolnum></citerefentry>
and <citerefentry><refentrytitle>tde-firewalld-tray</refentrytitle><manvolnum>1</manvolnum></citerefentry>.
</para>
</sect1>
</chapter>

<!-- ================================================================== -->

<chapter id="troubleshooting">
<title>Troubleshooting</title>

<qandaset id="faqlist">
<qandaentry>
<question><para>The window says <quote>The firewalld service is not running</quote>.</para></question>
<answer><para>
Click <guibutton>Start firewalld</guibutton>, or run
<userinput><command>sudo systemctl enable --now firewalld</command></userinput>.
If it does not start, see <userinput><command>journalctl -u firewalld</command></userinput>.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>

<qandaentry>
<question><para>Every change fails with <quote>Not authorized</quote>.</para></question>
<answer><para>
Make sure a &polkit; authentication agent is running
(<userinput><command>pgrep -a polkit-agent</command></userinput>) and that your
user is allowed to authenticate as an administrator, usually by being a
member of the <systemitem class="groupname">sudo</systemitem> group.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>

<qandaentry>
<question><para>My changes disappeared after a reboot.</para></question>
<answer><para>
You changed the runtime configuration. Click <guibutton>Runtime to
Permanent</guibutton>, or make the change again with the
<guilabel>Permanent</guilabel> configuration selected and reload the firewall.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>

<qandaentry>
<question><para>Changes to the permanent configuration have no effect.</para></question>
<answer><para>
The permanent configuration only takes effect after
<guibutton>Reload Firewall</guibutton>.
</para></answer>
</qandaentry>
</qandaset>
</chapter>

